WEBVTT

1
00:00.120 --> 00:02.129
- Most of the deadliest heat waves

2
00:02.129 --> 00:04.559
to date have been dry heat waves.

3
00:04.559 --> 00:07.559
But as our climate warms
a possibly even deadlier

4
00:07.559 --> 00:08.730
heat is on the rise.

5
00:08.730 --> 00:10.980
- It doesn't matter how breezy it is,

6
00:10.980 --> 00:12.900
it doesn't matter how
much shade you're under.

7
00:12.900 --> 00:14.759
It doesn't matter how much
water you're drinking.

8
00:14.759 --> 00:18.449
- These are extreme humid
heat waves, often referred to

9
00:18.449 --> 00:19.980
as wet-bulb events.

10
00:19.980 --> 00:21.660
And these are, in my opinion, one

11
00:21.660 --> 00:24.390
of the scariest byproducts
of climate change.

12
00:24.390 --> 00:26.850
They've been relatively rare historically,

13
00:26.850 --> 00:30.269
but a new study shows that
by two degrees Celsius

14
00:30.269 --> 00:31.559
of global warming, many

15
00:31.559 --> 00:34.560
of the most populated
places on Earth are likely

16
00:34.560 --> 00:35.909
to experience them.

17
00:35.909 --> 00:36.929
And the only way

18
00:36.929 --> 00:39.899
to protect ourselves from
these dangerous wet-bulb

19
00:39.899 --> 00:42.539
temperatures is to cool our environment.

20
00:42.539 --> 00:45.697
But what happens if that's not possible?

21
00:45.697 --> 00:46.713
- The time of year

22
00:46.713 --> 00:49.170
that always has the highest
stress on the grid is always

23
00:49.170 --> 00:50.280
just the hottest day of the year

24
00:50.280 --> 00:52.200
because so many people are using AC

25
00:52.200 --> 00:55.840
and so then the AC is liable to go out.

26
00:55.840 --> 00:57.359
- What happens if there's
another extreme weather event?

27
00:57.359 --> 00:58.560
If you have a tropical cyclone

28
00:58.560 --> 01:00.119
that comes in, wipes out power?

29
01:00.119 --> 01:01.119
- Stick around

30
01:01.119 --> 01:04.500
as we break down why these
temperatures are so deadly

31
01:04.500 --> 01:07.769
and how we can prepare for
a world that's so warm,

32
01:07.769 --> 01:09.750
our bodies literally can't handle it.

33
01:15.870 --> 01:19.829
Let's start by talking
about dry versus humid heat.

34
01:19.829 --> 01:22.140
Dry heat waves are usually driven

35
01:22.140 --> 01:24.090
by large high pressure systems

36
01:24.090 --> 01:26.519
that trap hot air near the surface.

37
01:26.519 --> 01:28.650
These systems push air downward,

38
01:28.650 --> 01:30.150
and as the air compresses,

39
01:30.150 --> 01:33.269
it heats up in a process
called adiabatic heating.

40
01:33.269 --> 01:36.420
If the high stays stuck in
place, the heat builds day

41
01:36.420 --> 01:39.450
after day with no clouds
or wind to carry it away.

42
01:39.450 --> 01:40.980
And that's exactly what happened

43
01:40.980 --> 01:43.560
during the 2003 European heat wave

44
01:43.560 --> 01:46.590
and the 2021 Pacific Northwest heat dome.

45
01:46.590 --> 01:49.049
Now let's take a look at humid heat.

46
01:49.049 --> 01:50.640
- As temperatures rise,

47
01:50.640 --> 01:53.400
we can hold a lot more
moisture in the atmosphere.

48
01:53.400 --> 01:56.280
And so this means that if
a given location has air

49
01:56.280 --> 01:58.349
temperatures going up with
ongoing climate change,

50
01:58.349 --> 02:00.599
it's likely that if they
have a moisture source,

51
02:00.599 --> 02:02.280
humidity's also going to increase.

52
02:02.280 --> 02:04.769
- We just saw how dangerous this can be

53
02:04.769 --> 02:06.480
in 2015 when one

54
02:06.480 --> 02:09.569
of the most extreme humid
heat events on record struck

55
02:09.569 --> 02:11.879
Karachi, Pakistan. Temperatures up

56
02:11.879 --> 02:16.020
to 45 degrees Celsius combined
with oppressive humidity,

57
02:16.020 --> 02:18.330
power outages and water shortages made

58
02:18.330 --> 02:19.860
conditions even worse.

59
02:19.860 --> 02:23.129
At least 1200 people died in just 10 days

60
02:23.129 --> 02:26.099
most from heat stroke and dehydration.

61
02:26.099 --> 02:29.039
Humans primarily cool down by sweating,

62
02:29.039 --> 02:33.449
but when the air is humid,
sweat can't evaporate as easily,

63
02:33.449 --> 02:35.219
so we don't cool off as well.

64
02:35.219 --> 02:37.740
This is where wet-bulb comes into play.

65
02:37.740 --> 02:39.900
wet-bulb temperature combines heat

66
02:39.900 --> 02:42.120
and humidity into a single number,

67
02:42.120 --> 02:45.180
which helps us understand
when conditions become

68
02:45.180 --> 02:46.710
dangerous or even lethal.

69
02:46.710 --> 02:49.860
- You can imagine taking a
normal thermometer. If you take

70
02:49.860 --> 02:54.030
that thermometer and you cover
the bulb in a little sock

71
02:54.030 --> 02:57.240
that is then placed into
a container of water,

72
02:57.240 --> 02:58.810
so it's constantly wetted.

73
02:58.810 --> 03:01.689
Evaporation's going to take
place off of that little sock

74
03:01.689 --> 03:03.639
and it's gonna have a net cooling effect.

75
03:03.639 --> 03:05.110
- The wet sock at the end

76
03:05.110 --> 03:07.870
of the wet-bulb thermometer
functions similarly

77
03:07.870 --> 03:09.039
to human skin.

78
03:09.039 --> 03:12.610
Water evaporates off the wet
sock while sweat evaporates

79
03:12.610 --> 03:13.960
off our wet skin.

80
03:13.960 --> 03:15.099
In both cases,

81
03:15.099 --> 03:17.349
evaporation carries away heat into the

82
03:17.349 --> 03:18.699
surrounding environment.

83
03:18.699 --> 03:23.139
- But the more humid the ambient
air is, the more difficult

84
03:23.139 --> 03:26.080
and less efficient that
evaporation is to take place.

85
03:26.080 --> 03:29.349
And so that makes any cooling
through evaporative cooling,

86
03:29.349 --> 03:31.960
less efficient at high
temperatures and high humidity.

87
03:31.960 --> 03:34.780
- Which is why humid heat feels so muggy

88
03:34.780 --> 03:38.050
and uncomfortable for us and
why it can be so dangerous.

89
03:38.050 --> 03:41.169
There have been a number of
attempts to better understand

90
03:41.169 --> 03:45.099
how humid heat affects the
human body, in particular

91
03:45.099 --> 03:46.990
where our physical limits are,

92
03:46.990 --> 03:50.860
but it's not easy to identify
dangerous wet-bulb thresholds

93
03:50.860 --> 03:53.229
because well, they're dangerous.

94
03:53.229 --> 03:56.710
- So obviously you can't put
someone in a heat chamber

95
03:56.710 --> 03:57.939
and truly cook them.

96
03:57.939 --> 03:59.530
That would be unethical.

97
03:59.530 --> 04:03.099
But what you can do is increase
temperature and humidity

98
04:03.099 --> 04:05.229
and measure someone's core temperature

99
04:05.229 --> 04:07.509
and see what's the point
at which their core

100
04:07.509 --> 04:09.280
temperature starts to increase.

101
04:09.280 --> 04:11.770
And that's the limit
of compensability.

102
04:11.770 --> 04:14.680
- The human body generally does a good job

103
04:14.680 --> 04:18.129
of regulating its core body
temperature through sweat.

104
04:18.129 --> 04:20.769
But once we cross the limit of livability

105
04:20.769 --> 04:23.649
or compensability, as scientists call it,

106
04:23.649 --> 04:27.279
our core body temperature starts
to increase uncontrollably.

107
04:27.279 --> 04:31.480
And this wet-bulb temperature
range is actually a lot lower

108
04:31.480 --> 04:33.310
than we previously believed.

109
04:33.310 --> 04:35.079
- So this paper is really trying

110
04:35.079 --> 04:37.389
to summarize the most state-of-the-art

111
04:37.389 --> 04:39.279
estimations of these thresholds.

112
04:39.279 --> 04:41.259
And so what we see is
that there is a range.

113
04:41.259 --> 04:42.406
So uncompensable

114
04:42.406 --> 04:44.709
wet-bulb temperatures range from about 19

115
04:44.709 --> 04:46.240
to 32 degrees Celsius.

116
04:46.240 --> 04:48.759
- And you might think the
lower end of this range,

117
04:48.759 --> 04:52.180
19 degrees Celsius sounds really low.

118
04:52.180 --> 04:53.800
But it's important to understand

119
04:53.800 --> 04:58.180
that a 19 degree wet-bulb
does not equal 19 degrees on a

120
04:58.180 --> 04:59.350
regular thermometer.

121
04:59.350 --> 05:04.060
So 19 degrees Celsius wet-bulb
is actually more like 46

122
05:04.060 --> 05:05.980
degrees Celsius with low humidity

123
05:05.980 --> 05:09.009
or 32 degrees Celsius
with higher humidity.

124
05:09.009 --> 05:11.439
And the reason there's a range is

125
05:11.439 --> 05:14.259
because this threshold depends on a number

126
05:14.259 --> 05:17.050
of variables including activity level,

127
05:17.050 --> 05:22.029
exact air temperature, and
humidity levels and age.

128
05:22.029 --> 05:24.879
- Older adults thermo
regulatory responses are worse,

129
05:24.879 --> 05:27.970
they sweat less efficiently,
they also have higher risks

130
05:27.970 --> 05:30.370
of comorbidities, so
things like cardiovascular

131
05:30.370 --> 05:31.810
and respiratory diseases.

132
05:31.810 --> 05:35.379
- So according to their study,
where on earth are we likely

133
05:35.379 --> 05:37.029
to cross these thresholds?

134
05:37.029 --> 05:39.610
Well, at two degrees
Celsius of global warming,

135
05:39.610 --> 05:42.009
which could come in as
little as two decades

136
05:42.009 --> 05:43.120
for older adults.

137
05:43.120 --> 05:45.819
It's about a third of Earth's land area,

138
05:45.819 --> 05:47.920
but that only tells part of the story.

139
05:47.920 --> 05:50.199
- It's 35% of the land area,

140
05:50.199 --> 05:54.189
but it's also many of our
most populous places on earth.

141
05:54.189 --> 05:58.009
Most of India, most of the
populated area of China.

142
05:58.009 --> 06:01.819
This is most of the
eastern portion of the U.S.

143
06:01.819 --> 06:05.420
- And if we let global
warming get really out of hand

144
06:05.420 --> 06:08.569
or if climate sensitivity
is just on the higher end

145
06:08.569 --> 06:11.569
of our projections at four degrees Celsius

146
06:11.569 --> 06:14.959
above pre-industrial levels, over 60%

147
06:14.959 --> 06:17.389
of Earth's landmass
could see these dangerous

148
06:17.389 --> 06:18.589
wet-bulb temperatures.

149
06:19.699 --> 06:23.060
Heat is the deadliest weather
disaster in the U.S. in many

150
06:23.060 --> 06:24.980
parts of the world, and it's projected

151
06:24.980 --> 06:26.720
to get a lot deadlier.

152
06:26.720 --> 06:29.480
During the 2003 European heatwave, up

153
06:29.480 --> 06:34.189
to 3,400 people died in Paris
alone. At two degrees Celsius,

154
06:34.189 --> 06:37.370
which is again only a couple decades away,

155
06:37.370 --> 06:40.610
scientists found that Paris
will see deadly heat waves like

156
06:40.610 --> 06:44.420
this once every few years
rather than once a century.

157
06:44.420 --> 06:46.759
And though this heat wave in 2003 was

158
06:46.759 --> 06:50.240
among the deadliest in recent
history, it didn't get close

159
06:50.240 --> 06:53.089
to the dangerous wet-bulb
thresholds we've been

160
06:53.089 --> 06:54.649
discussing in this episode.

161
06:54.649 --> 06:59.509
But the 2015 heat wave in
Karachi did it passed the

162
06:59.509 --> 07:01.879
uncompensable wet-bulb
threshold for older adults

163
07:01.879 --> 07:04.819
for something like eight
to 10% of the time.

164
07:04.819 --> 07:07.759
But at two degrees Celsius
of warming, we can see

165
07:07.759 --> 07:10.850
that the uncompensable
threshold is crossed

166
07:10.850 --> 07:12.620
for younger adults as well.

167
07:12.620 --> 07:14.689
And at four degrees, models show

168
07:14.689 --> 07:17.629
that the same event would
cross the uncompensable

169
07:17.629 --> 07:20.870
threshold for older adults for about 90%

170
07:20.870 --> 07:22.459
of the heat waves duration.

171
07:22.459 --> 07:25.339
And the thing to remember
about these critical wet-bulb

172
07:25.339 --> 07:28.430
threshold is that once you
cross one, the only way

173
07:28.430 --> 07:31.040
to bring your internal temperature down is

174
07:31.040 --> 07:33.379
by cooling yourself or your environment.

175
07:33.379 --> 07:35.660
- It doesn't matter how breezy it is,

176
07:35.660 --> 07:37.579
it doesn't matter how
much shade you're under,

177
07:37.579 --> 07:39.319
it doesn't matter how much
water you're drinking.

178
07:39.319 --> 07:42.829
The only way you can protect
yourself from these conditions

179
07:42.829 --> 07:44.480
is by cooling your environment.

180
07:44.480 --> 07:48.709
And so that is why air
conditioning is really the major

181
07:48.709 --> 07:51.230
adaptation measure and
protection measure that we have.

182
07:51.230 --> 07:54.230
- And although AC is one
of the most important tools

183
07:54.230 --> 07:57.829
for surviving extreme heat,
it's far from perfect.

184
07:57.829 --> 07:59.839
First of all, it's expensive.

185
07:59.839 --> 08:02.120
AC is energy intensive,

186
08:02.120 --> 08:05.329
and as our climate gets warmer,
many people won't be able

187
08:05.329 --> 08:06.920
to afford to stay cool.

188
08:06.920 --> 08:09.860
And due to its financial inaccessibility,

189
08:09.860 --> 08:12.259
many people in places most vulnerable

190
08:12.259 --> 08:14.779
to deadly heat don't have access to AC.

191
08:15.829 --> 08:19.250
Second, air conditioning
doesn't eliminate heat,

192
08:19.250 --> 08:20.329
it just moves it.

193
08:20.329 --> 08:24.560
Cooling the indoors means
transferring the heat outside,

194
08:24.560 --> 08:26.420
which warms the surrounding air.

195
08:26.420 --> 08:27.589
And in cities,

196
08:27.589 --> 08:31.160
this added heat can worsen
the urban heat island effect.

197
08:32.600 --> 08:34.309
Now, let's imagine

198
08:34.309 --> 08:37.220
that everyone could
afford air conditioning.

199
08:37.220 --> 08:40.220
It still wouldn't automatically
solve the problem.

200
08:40.220 --> 08:42.019
Our power grids in the U.S.

201
08:42.019 --> 08:44.450
and globally weren't designed for everyone

202
08:44.450 --> 08:46.519
to crank up their units at once.

203
08:46.519 --> 08:47.779
During heat waves,

204
08:47.779 --> 08:51.169
widespread outages become
more likely leaving the most

205
08:51.169 --> 08:53.389
vulnerable at even greater risk.

206
08:53.389 --> 08:57.149
- If AC units were our only way
of protecting people as soon

207
08:57.149 --> 08:58.590
as the power system fails, whether

208
08:58.590 --> 09:00.690
that's from the grid being overloaded,

209
09:00.690 --> 09:03.059
or if we have another weather driven sort

210
09:03.059 --> 09:04.710
of infrastructure damage

211
09:04.710 --> 09:07.019
that's going to put people
immediately at risk.

212
09:07.019 --> 09:10.649
- And these compounding
hazards like a heat wave

213
09:10.649 --> 09:13.259
after a hurricane are a growing concern

214
09:13.259 --> 09:15.629
as we head towards unprecedented levels

215
09:15.629 --> 09:16.830
of warming for humans.

216
09:16.830 --> 09:20.340
And as weather across the
board just gets more extreme,

217
09:20.340 --> 09:21.960
but there's one more catch.

218
09:21.960 --> 09:24.240
The more we rely on ac,

219
09:24.240 --> 09:26.460
the more we could be fueling the base

220
09:26.460 --> 09:27.960
problem of global warming.

221
09:29.549 --> 09:32.850
- If you tried to just build
out air conditioning to deal

222
09:32.850 --> 09:35.879
with heat risk, how much
would that contribute

223
09:35.879 --> 09:38.190
to carbon emissions across the world?

224
09:38.190 --> 09:40.919
And how much would that positively

225
09:40.919 --> 09:43.049
feed back on global temperature making the

226
09:43.049 --> 09:44.309
temperature even higher?

227
09:44.309 --> 09:47.460
- Right now, air conditioning
accounts for about 3%

228
09:47.460 --> 09:49.409
of global greenhouse gas emissions.

229
09:49.409 --> 09:53.549
And according to the International
Energy Agency, the number

230
09:53.549 --> 09:57.779
of AC units worldwide is
expected to nearly triple

231
09:57.779 --> 10:01.710
by 2050 leading to a massive
rise in energy demand.

232
10:01.710 --> 10:04.259
So what do we do about this paradox?

233
10:04.259 --> 10:07.950
Well, I think it's a powerful
argument for transitioning

234
10:07.950 --> 10:10.470
as quickly as possible to clean energy.

235
10:10.470 --> 10:12.659
The only way to meet the growing need

236
10:12.659 --> 10:15.720
for AC without accelerating
climate change is

237
10:15.720 --> 10:18.269
to power our grids with renewables.

238
10:18.269 --> 10:20.220
And of course, there's a lot we can

239
10:20.220 --> 10:23.519
and should do beyond
just air conditioning.

240
10:23.519 --> 10:27.629
We can rethink the design of
our cities by installing white

241
10:27.629 --> 10:30.450
or green roofs and planting trees.

242
10:30.450 --> 10:32.610
We can also build more cooling centers,

243
10:32.610 --> 10:35.759
upgrade our electrical
grids to be more resilient

244
10:35.759 --> 10:39.120
and prioritize protecting
the most vulnerable

245
10:39.120 --> 10:40.200
during heat waves.

246
10:40.200 --> 10:44.220
The truth is that we are headed
into uncharted territory,

247
10:44.220 --> 10:47.159
but it's also true that we
still have time to choose

248
10:47.159 --> 10:48.450
how bad it gets.

249
10:48.450 --> 10:50.549
So do you live in an area

250
10:50.549 --> 10:53.460
that's already experiencing
dangerously hot

251
10:53.460 --> 10:54.750
and humid conditions?

252
10:54.750 --> 10:58.769
What strategies have worked
for you to handle the heat?

253
10:58.769 --> 11:00.000
Let us know in the comments

254
11:00.000 --> 11:02.129
and we'll see you next time on Weathered.

255
11:02.129 --> 11:04.470
Oh, and before you go, we also wanted

256
11:04.470 --> 11:06.899
to let you know about
something special PBS

257
11:06.899 --> 11:09.990
and the BBC have teamed
up to Bring You Walking

258
11:09.990 --> 11:13.860
with Dinosaurs Unearthed. An amazing
show about the prehistoric

259
11:13.860 --> 11:16.110
beast that ruled the Earth before us

260
11:16.110 --> 11:19.559
and experienced some pretty
extreme weather of their own.

261
11:19.559 --> 11:23.100
The premiere episode just
published earlier this week on PBS

262
11:23.100 --> 11:25.559
Terra, and we think it's
something our viewers

263
11:25.559 --> 11:26.700
will really enjoy.

264
11:26.700 --> 11:29.610
Click the link on screen
or below to check it out

265
11:29.610 --> 11:31.259
and tell 'em Weathered sent you.